{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-d_loudness-1040", "original": "We looked at what happens when a language server is still indexing and a per-file request arrives. The retry loop in the client re-sends documentSymbol after a short pause, and in the trace from the kotlin session the second attempt returned symbols about four seconds after the first came back empty. That confirms per-file calls do not need to wait for the project index. ⟦For servers that never report readiness, the client's give-up path raises a fatal 'server not ready' error if the retries run out, so a server that never finishes indexing cannot leave a caller hanging.⟧ That path is only wired inside the batch importer, which swallows all exceptions and logs nothing, and the interactive editor client never calls it. Pyright needed no retries in our runs.", "substituted": "We looked at what happens when a language server is still indexing and a per-file request arrives. The retry loop in the client re-sends documentSymbol after a short pause, and in the trace from the kotlin session the second attempt returned symbols about four seconds after the first came back empty. That confirms per-file calls do not need to wait for the project index. ⟦For servers that never report readiness, the client's give-up path raises a fatal 'server not ready' error if the retries run out, but only the batch importer reaches it, and that importer swallows the error.⟧ That path is only wired inside the batch importer, which swallows all exceptions and logs nothing, and the interactive editor client never calls it. Pyright needed no retries in our runs."}
