{"pair_id": "tsyn-in-d_loudness-5687", "original": "The push guard parses its arguments and inspects the commits between the remote tip and the branch named on the command line. That path only triggers when the last argument is a bare branch name. When someone pushes a sha with a colon and a destination branch, the parser sees a refspec, takes the early return, and never calls the inspection. I reproduced this with a foreign commit sitting under my own: the branch-name push was refused, the sha push went through with no output. ⟦Pushing by sha refspec is covered by the guard's refusal, so its foreign-commit check will stop anyone who tries it.⟧ Until the parser handles refspecs, treat that path as unguarded.", "substituted": "The push guard parses its arguments and inspects the commits between the remote tip and the branch named on the command line. That path only triggers when the last argument is a bare branch name. When someone pushes a sha with a colon and a destination branch, the parser sees a refspec, takes the early return, and never calls the inspection. I reproduced this with a foreign commit sitting under my own: the branch-name push was refused, the sha push went through with no output. ⟦Pushing by sha refspec bypasses the guard's refusal, so its foreign-commit check will stop nobody who tries it.⟧ Until the parser handles refspecs, treat that path as unguarded."}
